# 3. Network Layer:
The Network Layer is the 3rd layer in the OSI model, sitting between the Data Link Layer and the Transport Layer.

It is responsible for source-to-destination delivery of packets across multiple networks.

The Network Layer determines the best path (routing) for data to travel from sender to receiver and handles logical addressing using IP addresses.

📌 Main Responsibilities
> Logical Addressing
Uses IP addresses to identify source and destination devices.
Example: IPv4, IPv6.

> Routing
Finds the best path for packets to travel.
Routers perform this function.

> Packet Forwarding
Moves packets from one network to another until they reach the destination.

> Fragmentation & Reassembly
Splits large packets into smaller fragments.
Reassembles them at the destination.

> Internetworking
Connects different networks together.

> Congestion Control
Helps reduce network traffic congestion.

📌 Advantages
Enables communication across multiple networks.
Uses logical addressing (IP).
Finds optimal paths using routing.
Supports large-scale networks like the Internet.

📌 Disadvantages
Routing can introduce delays.
More complex than lower layers.
Requires routing tables and protocols.


> Main Responsibilities of the Network Layer

>Logical Addressing
Every device connected to a network requires a unique logical address known as an IP address. The Network Layer assigns and uses these addresses to identify source and destination devices.

Routing

Routing is the process of determining the best path for data to travel from the sender to the receiver. Routers use routing algorithms and routing tables to make these decisions.

Packet Forwarding

After determining the route, the Network Layer forwards packets from one network to another until they reach the destination.

Fragmentation and Reassembly

Different networks support different packet sizes. If a packet is larger than the allowed size, it may be fragmented into smaller packets and reassembled at the destination.

Inter-Network Communication

The Network Layer enables communication between devices located on different networks and geographical locations.

> Internet Protocol (IP)
The primary protocol used at the Network Layer is the Internet Protocol (IP).

IP provides:
Logical addressing
Routing capabilities
Packet delivery services

The two major versions are:
IPv4 (Internet Protocol Version 4)
IPv6 (Internet Protocol Version 6)

> IPv4 (Internet Protocol Version 4)
IPv4 is the most widely used version of the Internet Protocol.

It uses a 32-bit addressing scheme, allowing approximately 4.3 billion unique addresses.

IPv4 addresses are written in dotted decimal notation, where the address is divided into four sections called octets.

> Characteristics of IPv4
Uses 32-bit addresses
Supports approximately 4.3 billion addresses
Uses decimal numbers
Widely deployed across the internet
Requires techniques like NAT due to address exhaustion

> Advantages of IPv4
Easy to implement
Widely supported
Mature and stable technology

>Limitations of IPv4
Limited address space
Increasing shortage of available addresses
Dependence on NAT
Less efficient routing compared to IPv6

> IPv4 Header
Every IPv4 packet contains a header that carries important control information.

Version
Indicates the IP version being used.

Header Length
Specifies the size of the header.

Total Length
Represents the total size of the packet.

Identification
Used during packet fragmentation and reassembly.

Time To Live (TTL)
Prevents packets from circulating indefinitely in the network.

Protocol
Identifies the upper-layer protocol such as TCP, UDP, or ICMP.

Source Address
Contains the sender's IP address.

Destination Address
Contains the receiver's IP address.

> IPv6 (Internet Protocol Version 6)
IPv6 was developed to overcome the limitations of IPv4, particularly address exhaustion.

IPv6 uses a 128-bit addressing scheme, providing an enormous number of unique addresses.

Addresses are written in hexadecimal format and separated by colons.

Characteristics of IPv6
Uses 128-bit addresses
Extremely large address space
Simplified header structure
Better support for security
Improved routing efficiency
Supports automatic address configuration
Advantages of IPv6
Vast Address Space

Provides enough addresses for future growth.

Improved Security

Designed with support for IPsec.

Efficient Routing

Simplified header processing improves performance.

Auto Configuration

Devices can automatically obtain addresses.

End-to-End Connectivity

Reduces dependency on NAT.

Challenges of IPv6
Migration from IPv4 can be complex.
Some legacy systems may not support IPv6.

> Classful Addressing
Classful addressing was the original method of dividing IPv4 addresses into predefined classes.

The purpose was to allocate network addresses based on organizational size.

The five classes are:

Class A
Designed for very large networks.

Class B
Designed for medium-sized organizations.

Class C
Designed for small organizations and local networks.

Class D
Reserved for multicast communication.

Class E
Reserved for research and experimental purposes.

> Limitations of Classful Addressing
Classful addressing led to inefficient utilization of IP addresses because organizations often received far more addresses than they actually needed.

Major problems included:-
Address wastage
Limited flexibility
Large routing tables
Poor scalability
-These limitations led to the development of CIDR.

> CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing)
CIDR is the modern method of IP address allocation.

Instead of fixed classes, CIDR allows network administrators to allocate addresses according to actual requirements.

CIDR uses a prefix length to indicate the network portion of an address.

> Benefits of CIDR
Efficient Address Allocation

Reduces address wastage.

Flexible Network Design

Networks can be created according to actual needs.

Better Scalability

Supports growth without unnecessary address allocation.

Route Aggregation

Reduces routing table size and improves routing efficiency.

> Public IP Address

A Public IP address is globally unique and can be accessed through the internet.

Public IP addresses are assigned by Internet Service Providers (ISPs).

>Characteristics
Globally unique
Routable on the internet
Assigned by ISPs
Required for internet-facing services
>Uses
Web servers
Cloud servers
Public websites
Online applications

> Private IP Address
A Private IP address is used within local networks and is not directly accessible from the internet.

These addresses can be reused in different organizations because they are not globally unique.

>Characteristics
Used within private networks
Not routable on the internet
Assigned by routers or network administrators
Conserves public IP addresses

>Common Uses
Home networks
Office networks
Educational institutions
Internal enterprise networks

> Public IP vs Private IP
Public IP
Accessible from the internet
Globally unique
Assigned by ISPs
Used for external communication
Private IP
Used within local networks
Not directly accessible from the internet
Can be reused by different organizations
Used for internal communication

> NAT (Network Address Translation)
NAT is a technique that allows multiple devices using private IP addresses to access the internet through a single public IP address.

It acts as a translator between private and public networks.

>Why NAT is Needed
Conserves public IP addresses
Enhances security
Allows private networks to access the internet

>Benefits of NAT
Reduced consumption of public IP addresses
Improved network security
Simplified network management

>Importance of the Network Layer

The Network Layer is crucial because it:

Enables communication across different networks
Provides logical addressing through IP addresses
Supports routing and path determination
Connects the global internet infrastructure
Ensures packets reach their intended destination efficiently
